title: One position per forest
tags: [[TreeRef forest]] [[Key invariants]]
kind: reference
summary: A metarecord holds at most one tree_ref row per field name, so every node has one path and a subtree hangs from one place.

A metarecord holds ''at most one position'' in a forest: at most one `tree_ref` row
per field name (`nothing` rows beside it are not positions). Every node therefore
has exactly one path, and a subtree hangs from one place. Any write that would give
a metarecord two is refused with `400` — a creation or whole-record overwrite
listing two, a multi-valued set, an append of a second one, a by-id edit or rename
turning another row into one:

```
metarecord 3f2a… would hold two positions in the 'path' forest: a metarecord has one position per forest (set the field to move it)
```

Moving a node is a `set` of its one position. A retype to `tree_ref` keeps the first
row of a metarecord that converts to a position and demotes the others to `nothing`,
reporting the metarecord like any value that fell back ([[Changing a field's type]]).
`mfr_path` is stricter still: a metarecord holds at most one `mfr_path` row of any
kind, `nothing` included — a file tracks one path.

The multi-map stays the rule for every other type. A file under several names (hard
links) is several metarecords, one per path, related by `mfr_inode`
([[What counts as a duplicate]]); a tag at several places of a hierarchy is a `ref`
from each place, not one node at several positions.

''Why'': a node at several positions has several paths but one set of children,
which then hang under one of them — an order no one sees, and one that moves the
whole subtree, unlogged, when that position goes. It was allowed until September
2026 and never used; with it went all the machinery that picked a "first position".
`mf repo check` names the metarecords an older daemon let hold several
([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]).
